+
+## Contract inventory
+
+Lines 1–134 define type-only API imports, extensible KnownApi/Api and provider IDs, separate image APIs/providers, tool choice, thinking levels and mappings, cache/transport/environment/header types. Provider strings are intentionally extensible; a string being representable does not prove an adapter exists. ThinkingLevelMap missing entries use provider defaults while null denotes unsupported. Model reasoning is therefore not just a boolean.
+
+Lines 135–288 describe request/stream/deferred options: AbortSignal, telemetry, API key, custom fetch, scoped env, payload/response callbacks, overriding/suppressing headers, timeout/retry/retry-delay limits, temperature, arbitrary OpenAI-compatible sampling parameters, maxTokens, transport, cache/session affinity and metadata. These are type contracts, not implementations of validation, cancellation, secure credential storage or retries. Arbitrary sampling parameters can override named payload fields by the documented source contract; a strict Rust implementation must deliberately decide its supported subset.
+
+Lines 289–369 map concrete API types to provider-specific options and define runtime dispatch shapes ProviderStreams and ProviderImages. Optional deferred methods indicate support, not guaranteed behavior for all wires. streamSimple reasoning/toolChoice/thinking budgets are provider-neutral input. The StreamFunction contract allows synchronous missing-auth errors before stream creation; after creation failures should terminate through the stream with error/aborted.
+
+Lines 370–498 define text/thinking/image/tool-call blocks, signatures and replay data, usage/cost, stop reasons and durable deferred handles; user/assistant/tool result message identities preserve API/provider/model plus concrete responseModel/responseId. Reasoning token usage is a subset of output, not an extra charge. Tool calls retain Google thoughtSignature and optional OpenAI namespace; thinking can be redacted while retaining opaque replay data. Tool results may carry images and newly available tool names. Images generation has a distinct context/result/stop contract.
+
+Lines 499–580 describe tool schemas and constrained sampling: JSON schema strict prefer/require versus grammar variants, Context system/messages/tools, and the event union. Event order requires start before updates/done; setup can emit error directly. Each content block has start/delta/end, tool argument deltas are provider-specific, and partial is shared mutable response-so-far rather than an immutable event-time snapshot. A consumer must not treat intermediate tool arguments as final or normal EOF as a terminal done.
+
+Lines 581–723 enumerate OpenAI completion/Responses compatibility. These include store/developer/reasoning/usage/finish-reason support, token field choice, tool-history shape, thinking serialization, chat-template variables, routing preferences, grammar/strict tools, caches, session affinity and optional explicit maximum output. Some completion compatibility permits inferring stop on EOF; zenpi's stricter Stage 1 terminal requirement intentionally rejects that fallback. URL autodetection mentioned in comments is performed elsewhere.
+
+Lines 724–786 cover Anthropic and Bedrock compatibility: eager tool input streaming, cache markers, session affinity, temperature, adaptive effort, signatures, strict tools, effort binding, permitted refusal fallback targets and deferred tools. This file does not itself implement any native adapter.
+
+Lines 787–850 describe OpenRouter and Vercel routing (provider allow/deny, data handling, ordering, quantization, price/latency/throughput constraints) and pricing rates/tiers. Cost rates are dollars per million tokens; a matching input threshold applies a tier to the whole request. These numbers have no source/version fields in this type.
+
+Lines 851–881 define Model and ImagesModel: exact id/name/api/provider/baseUrl, reasoning and optional level map, input text/image, cost, contextWindow, maxTokens, sampling parameters, headers and conditional API-specific compat. Image model output modalities are separate and reasoning/context limits are omitted. Model type has no generic file-input or structured-output capability flag, nor a per-model general tool/stream boolean. Those desired zenpi fields must be supported extensions and intersected with actual adapter behavior.
+
+## Target comparison
+
+Baseline zenpi src/backend.rs ProviderCapabilities::for_wire_api grants broad capabilities solely from Chat versus Responses, including all Responses models as image/file/structured/reasoning capable. It has no model context/output metadata or reasoning-level membership and accepts model names as bounded opaque strings. ConfigFile/model_catalog returns provider profiles, not exact model records with provenance. This makes advertised wire support insufficient for validating a chosen model.
+
+ZS1-107 should distinguish an implemented wire envelope from a source/version-tagged model descriptor; intersect the two and apply explicit user overrides with field-level provenance. Missing capabilities should not become true, absent context should have a conservative finite fallback, and unknown model identity must not silently map to another known model. Current existing Responses input/file/structured paths must remain usable when explicitly configured/supported. No secret/header values belong in model listings.
+
